# Task 1 — Distributed Web Infrastructure

## Infrastructure overview

Three-server infrastructure hosting www.foobar.com, with a load balancer
distributing traffic across two identical servers and a MySQL Primary-Replica
database cluster.

---

## Why each additional element?

- **Load balancer (HAProxy)**: distributes incoming traffic across multiple
  servers so no single server is overwhelmed, and provides redundancy at the
  application layer.
- **2nd server**: if Server 1 fails, Server 2 continues serving requests.
  Redundancy eliminates the single point of failure at the server level.
- **MySQL Primary-Replica cluster**: separates read and write operations for
  better performance and provides a copy of the data in case the Primary fails.

---

## Load balancer distribution algorithm

HAProxy is configured with the **Round Robin** algorithm. Each new incoming
request is forwarded to the next server in turn:
request 1 → Server 1, request 2 → Server 2, request 3 → Server 1, and so on.
This distributes the load evenly when both servers have equal capacity.

---

## Active-Active vs Active-Passive

This setup uses **Active-Active**: both servers receive and handle traffic
simultaneously. Resources on both machines are used at all times, which
maximises throughput.

**Active-Passive** is different: one server handles all traffic while the
other sits idle, ready to take over only if the active server fails.
Active-Passive wastes resources but can be simpler to manage.

---

## How Primary-Replica (Master-Slave) works

The Primary node accepts all write operations (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE). Every
change is recorded in the binary log and replicated asynchronously to the
Replica node. The Replica keeps an up-to-date copy of the data and handles
read queries to offload the Primary.

---

## Primary node vs Replica node — from the application's perspective

- **Primary**: the application sends all writes here. This is the source of
  truth for the database.
- **Replica**: the application can send read queries here to reduce load on
  the Primary. However, the Replica cannot accept writes — sending a write to
  it would either be rejected or cause data inconsistency.

---

## Issues with this infrastructure

### SPOF
The load balancer itself is a single point of failure. If HAProxy goes down,
no request reaches either server and the site is completely unavailable.

### Security issues
There is no firewall — every port on every machine is exposed. There is no
HTTPS — traffic travels in plain text and can be intercepted.

### No monitoring
There is no alerting system. If a server crashes, a disk fills up, or
response times spike, nobody is notified automatically.
